{"kind":"stack","id":"moon-stack","title":"The Moon and the Earth","theme":{"background":"#0b1d3a","foreground":"#ffffff","font":"Arial, sans-serif"}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c1","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c1-t","content":"Orbit"},{"type":"text","id":"c1-e","content":"An orbit is the curved path one object takes around another, like the Moon going round the Earth."},{"type":"button","id":"c1-next","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c2","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"image","picto":"moon"},{"type":"text","id":"c2-q","content":"What does the Moon travel around?"},{"type":"button","id":"c2-a","label":"The Earth","why":"The Moon orbits the Earth, so the Earth is what it travels around.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c2-b","label":"The Sun","why":"Pupils often think the Moon orbits the Sun, as the Earth does.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c2-c","label":"Mars","why":"Pupils may mix up the Moon with planets they have heard of.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c3","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c3-t","content":"Rotation"},{"type":"text","id":"c3-e","content":"Rotation is when an object spins around its own centre, like the Earth turning once every day."},{"type":"button","id":"c3-next","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c4","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c4-q","content":"How long does the Moon take to travel once around the Earth?"},{"type":"button","id":"c4-a","label":"About 27 days","why":"The Moon takes about 27 days to complete one orbit of the Earth.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c4-b","label":"About 1 day","why":"Pupils confuse the Moon's orbit with the Earth's daily spin.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c4-c","label":"About 365 days","why":"Pupils mix up the Moon's orbit with the Earth's yearly orbit of the Sun.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c5","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c5-q","content":"Which way does the Moon go round the Earth?"},{"type":"button","id":"c5-a","label":"In a near-circle","why":"The Moon follows a roughly circular path around the Earth.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c6"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c5-b","label":"In a straight line","why":"Pupils may think the Moon just flies off in a straight line.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c6"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c5-c","label":"Up and down only","why":"Pupils may picture the Moon only rising and setting, not travelling around.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c6"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c6","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c6-t","content":"You are an astronaut on the Moon. Do you look back at the Earth or the Sun first?"},{"type":"button","id":"c6-a","label":"Look at the Earth","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c6-b","label":"Look at the Sun","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c7","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c7-t","content":"The Earth hangs in the sky, big and blue. It stays in almost the same place because the Moon spins at the same rate it orbits."},{"type":"button","id":"c7-next","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c8","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c8-t","content":"The Sun is bright and hot. Its light shows the Moon's shape changing as it travels around the Earth."},{"type":"button","id":"c8-next","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c9","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c9-q","content":"What makes the Moon's shape seem to change?"},{"type":"button","id":"c9-a","label":"How much of its lit side we see from Earth","why":"As the Moon orbits, we see different amounts of its sunlit side, giving phases.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c9-b","label":"The Earth's shadow growing and shrinking","why":"Earth's shadow does sometimes fall on the Moon, but that is an eclipse, not the usual phases.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c9-c","label":"The Moon changing its actual size","why":"Pupils may think the Moon really gets bigger and smaller.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c10","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c10-q","content":"How many times does the Moon orbit the Earth in one year?"},{"type":"button","id":"c10-a","label":"About 12 times","why":"Each orbit takes about 27 days, so roughly 12 orbits fit in a year.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c10-b","label":"Once","why":"Pupils may think the Moon's orbit matches the Earth's yearly orbit.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c10-c","label":"About 100 times","why":"Pupils may think the orbit is much faster than it really is.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c11","kind":"end","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c11-h","content":"Well done, you finished the Moon and the Earth!"},{"type":"text","id":"c11-m","content":"You now know how the Moon orbits the Earth, how long it takes, and why its shape seems to change."}],"actions":[]}}
